  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "0b3d6cec-b7e1-4daf-a171-c8acbe1c30fb",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## (III - 2) Select and clean relevant columns for analysis\n",
    "*Explanation:*\n",
    "\n",
    "* Constructs a subset of columns: retaining `peptide`, the original `proteins` annotation, `q_value`, and all other columns except technical metadata (`charge`, `score`, `spectral_angle`) which are not needed for quantification.\n",
    "* The `proteins` field is in UniProt format like `sp|P12345|PROT_NAME;tr|P12345|PROT_NAME;...`.\n",
    "* For each peptide make a row copy for every protein it could possibly map to.\n",
    "* Drops any peptides that failed to parse into a valid UniProt accession.\n",
    "  \n",
    "*Why:*\n",
    "Reduces clutter and focuses downstream computation on actual intensity data and needed identifiers.  \n",
    "\n",
    "*Teaching note:* Students can be asked what happens if peptides map to multiple proteins (this script doesn’t resolve shared peptides explicitly—it aggregates all evidence under each protein) and discuss how “razor” or parsimony approaches differ."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 3,
   "id": "fdb1c161-554f-43d0-90e8-ff85a8b0b95c",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "name": "stdout",
     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "First few rows of the cleaned and expanded DataFrame:\n",
      "              peptide   q_value  Soybean_LPBiomaker_HP2.mzML  \\\n",
      "0  SM[+15.9949]AQIALR  0.007627                 6.214107e+06   \n",
